Moreover, in addition to the total PDFs, we calculated from the MD trajectories the PDFs for specific atom–atom correlations, which provide greater understanding of the salient real-space structural movements. We plot in Fig. 2a–c the partial radial distribution functions \( g_{ij}(r) \) for \( \mathrm{Zn-N} \), \( \mathrm{Zn-Im} \) (where \( \mathrm{Im} \) is the centre of mass of the imidazolate group) and \( \mathrm{Zn-Zn} \) pairs. In addition to the overall thermal broadening of the peaks, there is a clear loss of long-range order at high temperature. At intermediate temperatures (around \( 1{,}000\,\mathrm{K} \)), the system has liquid-like disorder, with \( g_{ij}(r) \) that do not go to zero after the first peak, although it retains some order at distances larger than \( 10\,\mathrm{\AA} \). To characterize further this state, we plot in Fig. 2d the generalized Lindemann ratio24, computed from the width of the first peak in the \( \mathrm{Zn-N} \) and \( \mathrm{Zn-Zn} \) partial radial distribution functions. The usual criterion used to determine melting from the Lindemann ratio is between 10 and \( 15\% \), which indicates in our case a melting temperature between 1,000 and \( 1{,}500\,\mathrm{K} \)—and we note that the \( \mathrm{Zn-Zn} \) ratio shows a clear disruption in slope at \( 1{,}200\,\mathrm{K} \).
## Thermodynamics of melting
Approaching the phenomenon from a thermodynamic standpoint, we plot in Fig. 2e the evolution of heat capacity \( C_{\mathrm{V}} \) as a function of temperature. We see a clear jump in the heat capacity, indicative of a solid–liquid phase transition, from the value of the crystal phase \( (C_{\mathrm{V}}\approx2.2\,\mathrm{J}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\,\mathrm{K}^{-1}) \) to a higher value for the ZIF liquid \( (C_{\mathrm{V}}\approx2.8\,\mathrm{J}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\,\mathrm{K}^{-1}) \). Integration yields an estimate for the enthalpy of fusion of the ZIF of \( \Delta H_{\mathrm{fus}}=173\,\mathrm{J}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1} \), which is in line with values
for materials such as quartz \( (\Delta H_{\mathrm{fus}} = 146 \, \mathrm{J} \, \mathrm{g}^{-1}) \) and cristobalite \( (\Delta H_{\mathrm{fus}} = 237 \, \mathrm{J} \, \mathrm{g}^{-1}) \)
